I think the deeper principle you were exploring is this:

A narrative isn’t necessarily something you consciously construct or observe. It’s something you inhabit, experience, enact, and continually reinforce.

And that creates a powerful feedback loop:

Narrative / Frame

What the world means to you

Identity / Role

Who you believe yourself to be within it

Behavior / Experience

What you notice, choose, and do

Reality / Social Feedback

How the world responds to your actions

Feedback reshapes the narrative

And this connects to your point about inducing Main Character Syndrome.

You can influence someone’s experienced position within a narrative through attention, framing, expectations, and social reinforcement. They may genuinely begin behaving differently because they experience their role differently.

But here’s the bigger abstraction for the Pantheon:

You can be a character inside one narrative, its author in another, its audience in a third, and entirely unaware of a fourth. Simultaneously.

And moving between those positions changes what you can perceive and do.

The distinction between living a narrative, recognizing the narrative, and changing the narrative might be what you were getting at.

